  {
    "id": "10.64898/2026.03.26.714097",
    "title": "Integrated omics analysis reveals reorganization of nitrogen and lipid metabolism in a toluene-degrading bacterium in the gas phase",
    "authors": [
      "Inoue, S.",
      "Naobayashi, T.",
      "Tokiyoshi, K.",
      "Yoshimoto, S.",
      "Tsugawa, H.",
      "Hori, K."
    ],
    "summary": "Gas-phase bioprocesses, in which microbial cells are immobilized on solid carriers exposed to the gas phase, enable efficient conversion of poorly water-soluble compounds for biodegradation and bioproduction. However, operation without a surrounding bulk aqueous medium can constrain the replenishment of water and nonvolatile nutrients. The metabolic mechanisms that sustain cellular activity in these low-water environments remain largely unknown. Here, we elucidated the metabolic alterations in the highly adhesive bacterium Acinetobacter sp. Tol 5 degrading toluene in the gas and aqueous phases. Polyurethane carriers bearing cells from independent cultures were assigned to the gas phase or to two aqueous media, nitrogen-depleted basal salt medium and phosphate-buffered saline, so that the conditions differed only in phase and nutrient availability. In cells actively degrading toluene, integrated metabolomics, lipidomics, and transcriptomics (97 metabolites, 108 lipids, and 3,899 genes) revealed a metabolic state specific to the gas phase. Under gas-phase conditions, amino acid and nucleotide degradation was enhanced while the intracellular glutamate pool was maintained at a high level, and citrulline specifically accumulated. Membrane lipids were remodeled, and the storage lipid triacylglycerol was consumed, suggesting that it serves as an endogenous carbon and energy source and may also contribute to metabolic water production under nutrient- and water-limited gas-phase conditions. These findings highlight nitrogen metabolism and lipid mobilization as features of gas-phase adaptation, providing a rational basis for designing robust gas-phase bioprocesses and selecting suitable microbial chassis.",
    "published": "2026-11-07T00:00:00+00:00",
    "updated": null,
    "source": "bioRxiv",
    "link": "https://www.biorxiv.org/content/10.64898/2026.03.26.714097",
    "doi": "10.64898/2026.03.26.714097",
    "categories": [],
    "keywords_matched": [
      "\\bLipid\\ metabolism\\b"
    ]
  },
  {
    "id": "10.1101/2024.04.08.588540",
    "title": "Glycolytic enzymes form membrane-less condensates in the malaria parasite Plasmodium falciparum by sensing glucose levels",
    "authors": [
      "Ishii, R.",
      "Sakura, T.",
      "Inaoka, D. K.",
      "Tokumasu, F."
    ],
    "summary": "Recent studies have shown that liquid-liquid phase separation (LLPS) in cells can regulate essential cellular events, including metabolic processes. Glycolytic bodies (G-bodies) are biomolecular condensates formed through the LLPS of glycolytic enzymes, and they accelerate glycolysis to overcome hypoxic stress in several organisms. Although the asexual blood stage (ABS) of the human malaria parasite Plasmodium falciparum highly depends on glycolysis for energy production, there have been no reports of the formation of such G-bodies throughout the parasite's lifecycle. Using fluorescence tagging and live imaging, we found that G-body-like condensates containing phosphofructokinase 9 (PFK9) and phosphoglycerate kinase (PGK) were formed in the parasite cells after long-term culture under conditions of low glucose. These G-body-like structures appeared stable, but membrane staining and osmotic stress experiments suggested that the observed condensates were not associated with a lipid membrane. Notably, PFK9 and PGK formed spatially distinct, non-overlapping condensates, indicating that these structures were formed with different mechanism as compared to the shared, multi-enzyme G-bodies described in other organisms. Whether the condensation of these enzymes enhances or restrains glycolysis under low-glucose stress remains to be determined. These results suggest that the formation of glycolytic enzyme condensates may be an enzyme-specific cellular response that helps the parasite adapt to reductions in blood glucose levels in the host during the ABS.",
    "published": "2026-11-07T00:00:00+00:00",
    "updated": null,
    "source": "bioRxiv",
    "link": "https://www.biorxiv.org/content/10.1101/2024.04.08.588540",
    "doi": "10.1101/2024.04.08.588540",
    "categories": [],
    "keywords_matched": [
      "\\bGlycolysis\\b"
    ]
  },
  {
    "id": "10.64898/2026.11.06.764689",
    "title": "SPACE-Tag Enables High-Throughput Spatial Chromatin Profiling With CUT&Tag",
    "authors": [
      "Yan, C.",
      "He, R.",
      "Fernandez, S.",
      "Pleska, M.",
      "Maniatis, S.",
      "Raimondi, I.",
      "Vickovic, S."
    ],
    "summary": "Spatial epigenomics maps chromatin states within intact tissues, yet current methods remain limited by accessibility and scale. We developed SPACE-Tag, a spatial CUT&Tag framework that converts epigenetic marks-associated DNA into polyadenylated RNA through T7 based linear amplification, enabling direct integration with standard spatial transcriptomic workflows. Applied to the mouse brain, SPACETag resolved activating and repressive chromatin landscapes, recovered region-specific gene regulatory programs across anatomical domains, and showed strong correlation with existing bulk and single cell reference data. We generated an integrated multimodal atlas of the aging mouse brain, profiling five histone modifications and the transcriptome in parallel across the adult lifespan to establish the largest spatial multimodal dataset to date. This analysis uncovered sharp regional divergence with aging in the brain: fiber tracts showed widespread transcriptional downregulation, often without nearby chromatin state transitions, whereas the isocortex showed coordinated chromatin transcriptome changes, including an H3K4me1 peak module that increased with age, was enriched at super-enhancers and linked to upregulation of neuronal plasticity regulators. These findings highlight the power of multimodal and high throughput spatial genomics in uncovering the divergent regulatory logic that governs tissue-specific vulnerability and resilience in the aging brain.",
    "published": "2026-11-07T00:00:00+00:00",
    "updated": null,
    "source": "bioRxiv",
